---
import logos from '../assets/img/inline-logos.svg';

const questions = [
  {
    q: "What's actually creating a barrier?",
    body: 'Not just friction in the UI sense, but includes accessibility, socioeconomic access, and constraints the user may not say out loud.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are their developmental needs?',
    body: "People aren't a monolith across a lifespan, a role, or a level of experience. K12 is a perfect example of varying needs across ages, but this extends to adults too.",
  },
  {
    q: "What's their capacity to engage?",
    body: "Wanting the outcome and having the bandwidth to reach it today aren't the same. Design that ignores this asks people to perform readiness they may not have.",
  },
];

const process = [
  {
    step: 'Discover',
    body: "Ask the right questions, then test whether they're the right ones. Read the research, read the market, pay attention to what's happening outside the building.",
  },
  {
    step: 'Define',
    body: 'Decide what success means, and be rigorous about it before anything gets built. A metric chosen after launch is a metric chosen to look good.',
  },
  {
    step: 'Scope',
    body: "Don't try to solve the world. Pick a few problems, solve them properly, and build from there. Scope creep is how good products become mediocre ones.",
  },
  {
    step: 'Prototype',
    body: "With the people who'll build and deliver it in the room — engineering, product, and the subject matter experts who own the domain. Ship to a local sandbox or something like Vercel, leveraging AI-friendly design systems. The prototype linked in my Experience case study was built this way, and so were the projects below.",
  },
  {
    step: 'Test',
    body: 'A frontline user in their actual environment, mid-task, distracted, three other things demanding attention, tells you more than a recruited participant behind glass with a clean hour set aside. Both are useful. The second is rare and worth arranging.',
  },
];
---

<section class="how" data-reveal>
  <div class="section-label">how I work</div>

  <div class="how-part">
    <div class="part-head">
      <h2>The Heart</h2>
      <p>
        There are many ways to approach problem solving from a design perspective. But there are
        three questions I keep at the heart of my own process. They hold true regardless of
        population, and they stem from my own values as a helper.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="questions">
      {questions.map(({ q, body }) => (
        <li class="question">
          <h3>{q}</h3>
          <p>{body}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <p class="transition">
      Those questions shape what gets built. This is how it actually gets built.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="how-part how-part-split">
    <div class="part-head">
      <h2>The Logistics</h2>
      <p>
        Values only matter if they survive contact with a business. Influence, storytelling, and
        clear communication are what let a design decision actually ship, not just get approved in a
        meeting. That means knowing the tech stack you're designing into, how the product is
        actually sold, and where the real constraints are, typically, budget, timeline, or politics.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="logo-strip">
      <img src={logos.src} alt="Figma, Claude, Cursor, Astro, and GitHub" width={logos.width} height={logos.height} />
      <figcaption>an example workflow I use</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="how-part">
    <div class="part-head">
      <h2>The Process</h2>
    </div>

    <dl class="steps">
      {process.map(({ step, body }) => (
        <div class="step">
          <dt>{step}</dt>
          <dd>{body}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <p class="how-note">
      One thing that has genuinely changed: the handoff got shorter. Work that used to live in Figma
      now often goes straight into code against a design system, far less to translate and
      considerably more time for edge cases and polish. It also makes the Discover and Define phases
      arguably more important, not less. When execution is faster, the cost of solving the wrong
      problem well goes up.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .how {
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 72px 48px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 48px;
  }

  .how-part {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 28px;
  }

  /* Logistics runs beside the logo strip rather than full width. */
  .how-part-split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 380px;
    gap: 56px;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .logo-strip {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
  }

  .logo-strip img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    display: block;
  }

  .logo-strip figcaption {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 13.5px;
    font-style: italic;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .part-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
    max-width: 600px; 
  }

  .how h2 {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: clamp(26px, 3.2vw, 36px);
    font-weight: 300;
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .part-head p {
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* ---- the three questions ---- */
  .questions {
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--ink-faint);
    border: 0.5px solid var(--ink-faint);
    counter-reset: question;
  }

  .question {
    background: var(--warm-white);
    padding: 28px 24px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
    counter-increment: question;
  }

  .question h3 {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 21px;
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .question h3::before {
    content: counter(question, decimal-leading-zero);
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    color: var(--teal);
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .question p {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .transition {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: clamp(18px, 2vw, 22px);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 300;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 820px;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* ---- process ---- */
  .steps {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  .step {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 210px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 32px;
    padding: 18px 0;
    border-top: 0.5px solid var(--ink-faint);
  }

  .step:last-child {
    border-bottom: 0.5px solid var(--ink-faint);
  }

  .step dt {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 20px;
    color: var(--ink);
    line-height: 1.25;
  }

  .step dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .how-note {
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    max-width: 860px;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .how-part-split {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 28px;
      align-items: start;
    }
    .logo-strip { max-width: 420px; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .how { padding: 56px 24px; gap: 36px; }
    .step { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 6px; }
  }
</style>
